(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-12-04"] = {"phy-12-04-m01":{"idea":"The zeroth law is the reason a thermometer works. It says that bodies in thermal equilibrium share one property.","steps":["Suppose body A and body B are each in thermal equilibrium with body C.","The zeroth law says A and B are then in equilibrium with each other.","So there must be one quantity that is equal for all three bodies.","We call that quantity temperature. Body C acts as the thermometer."],"others":"Internal energy is defined by the first law, not the zeroth. Entropy comes from the second law. Work is a way of transferring energy and is not a property that bodies share at equilibrium.","trap":"Zeroth law gives temperature, first law gives energy, second law gives entropy.","answer":"temperature"},"phy-12-04-m02":{"idea":"The first law is energy conservation. The sign rule decides what W means.","steps":["The first law is ΔU = Q − W.","Q is the heat given to the system.","W is the work done by the system on its surroundings.","W is positive when the gas expands and pushes something outwards."],"others":"The total energy of the system is U plus other terms, not W. Heat lost by the system is a negative Q, not W. Work done on the system would carry the opposite sign: it would be +W in the equation ΔU = Q + W.","trap":"In ΔU = Q − W, work done by the gas is positive. In ΔU = Q + W, work done on the gas is positive.","answer":"the work done by the system"},"phy-12-04-m03":{"idea":"An ideal gas has no forces between molecules, so its internal energy is only kinetic.","steps":["Internal energy is the total molecular energy.","With no molecular forces, there is no potential energy.","So U is the sum of the kinetic energies, and this depends on temperature alone.","For a monatomic gas, U = (3/2) n R T."],"others":"Pressure and volume together can change while T stays fixed, and U does not change then. Volume alone and pressure alone fail for the same reason: neither sets the kinetic energy of the molecules.","trap":"Constant T means ΔU = 0 for an ideal gas, whatever else happens.","answer":"temperature"},"phy-12-04-m04":{"idea":"In an isothermal process the temperature does not change, so the internal energy of an ideal gas does not change either.","steps":["Isothermal means T is constant.","For an ideal gas, U depends only on T, so ΔU = 0.","The first law ΔU = Q − W gives 0 = Q − W.","So Q = W: the gas takes in as much heat as the work it does."],"others":"Minus the work would mean Q = −W, which is the reverse sign. Zero heat is wrong: heat flows in to pay for the work. Equal to ΔU would make Q zero, because ΔU is zero here.","trap":"No change of temperature does not mean no heat flow. Heat goes in and comes out as work.","answer":"equal to the work W done by the gas"},"phy-12-04-m05":{"idea":"Adiabatic means no heat enters or leaves the gas. All energy change comes from work.","steps":["Adiabatic: Q = 0.","The first law gives ΔU = −W.","If the gas expands, W is positive, so ΔU is negative and the temperature falls.","If it is compressed, the gas warms up."],"others":"W = 0 with a constant volume describes an isochoric process. ΔU = 0 with constant temperature describes an isothermal process. Q = W with constant pressure is not a standard rule for any of these.","trap":"Adiabatic is not isothermal. The temperature changes.","answer":"Q = 0, so ΔU = −W, and the gas cools when it expands"},"phy-12-04-m06":{"idea":"In a constant-volume process, the gas cannot push on anything, so it does no work.","steps":["Work done by a gas is W = pΔV.","At constant volume, ΔV = 0, so W = 0.","The first law then gives ΔU = Q.","All the heat goes into internal energy, Q = nC_vΔT."],"others":"ΔU = 0 would hold only if the temperature did not change, and it does. Constant pressure belongs to the isobaric process. Q = 0 is the adiabatic case and has nothing to do with volume being fixed.","trap":"Isochoric: W = 0. Isothermal: ΔU = 0. Adiabatic: Q = 0.","answer":"W = 0, so all the heat added goes into the internal energy"},"phy-12-04-m07":{"idea":"On a p–V diagram, the work done is the area under the curve. For a closed loop, the net work is the area inside.","steps":["On the way out, the gas expands and does positive work.","On the way back, the gas is compressed and does negative work.","The two areas under the curves do not cancel; what is left is the area inside the loop.","That area is the net work per cycle."],"others":"The change in internal energy over a cycle is zero, but the work is not. The area under the highest curve alone ignores the work done on the way back. Net work is not zero, since the loop has a non-zero area.","trap":"After one full cycle ΔU = 0, so net Q = net W.","answer":"the area enclosed by the loop on the p–V diagram"},"phy-12-04-m08":{"idea":"In a free expansion, the gas expands into empty space and pushes against nothing.","steps":["The gas meets no pressure, so W = 0.","The container is insulated, so Q = 0.","The first law gives ΔU = Q − W = 0.","For an ideal gas, ΔU = 0 means the temperature is unchanged."],"others":"Q = W and ΔU = 0 fits an isothermal expansion, where W is not zero. Positive W and negative ΔU describes a reversible adiabatic expansion against a piston. Positive Q and ΔU would need heat flowing in.","trap":"Free expansion is irreversible. It is not isothermal in the usual sense, because the pressure is not defined during the process.","answer":"Q = 0, W = 0 and ΔU = 0"},"phy-12-04-m09":{"idea":"γ depends on the degrees of freedom. A diatomic gas has 5 at ordinary temperature.","steps":["Diatomic molecule: 3 translations and 2 rotations, so f = 5.","C_v = (5/2)R and C_p = C_v + R = (7/2)R.","γ = C_p/C_v = (7/2)/(5/2) = 7/5.","This equals 1.4, the value for air."],"others":"4/3 is the value for a non-linear polyatomic gas. 5/3 is the value for a monatomic gas. 9/7 would need 7 degrees of freedom, as if vibration were active.","trap":"Monatomic 5/3, diatomic 7/5.","answer":"7/5"},"phy-12-04-m10":{"idea":"Mayer's relation shows how much more heat a gas needs at constant pressure than at constant volume.","steps":["At constant volume, heat only raises internal energy: Q = C_v ΔT.","At constant pressure, heat also pays for the work done by the gas.","For one mole, this work is pΔV = RΔT.","So C_p = C_v + R, or C_p − C_v = R."],"others":"C_p + C_v = R would make C_p less than R. C_p/C_v = R confuses the dimensionless γ with a constant that has units. C_p × C_v = R has the wrong units.","trap":"C_p − C_v = R has the units J/(mol K) on both sides.","answer":"C_p − C_v = R"},"phy-12-04-m11":{"idea":"On a p–V diagram, the adiabat falls faster than the isotherm. The slope tells us why.","steps":["Isotherm: pV = constant. Differentiating gives slope = −p/V.","Adiabat: pV^γ = constant. Differentiating gives slope = −γp/V.","Both slopes use the same p and V at the common point.","Since γ is greater than 1, the adiabat slope is larger in size, so it is steeper."],"others":"Horizontal and vertical are not the shapes of these curves. Equally steep would need γ = 1. Less steep would need γ below 1, which never happens for a gas.","trap":"Adiabats are steeper by the factor γ.","answer":"steeper"},"phy-12-04-m12":{"idea":"Isothermal work needs an integral, because the pressure falls as the gas expands.","steps":["p = nRT/V, so W = ∫ p dV = nRT ∫ dV/V.","The limits go from V to 2V.","W = nRT ln(2V/V) = nRT ln 2.","Since ln 2 ≈ 0.693, W ≈ 0.693 nRT."],"others":"2nRT treats the pressure as constant, which it is not. nRT/2 has no basis in the integral. nRT with no logarithm drops the ln of the volume ratio, and ln 2 is not 1.","trap":"Isothermal work contains ln(V₂/V₁). Doubling the volume gives ln 2.","answer":"nRT ln 2"},"phy-12-04-m13":{"idea":"The Kelvin–Planck statement says that a heat engine cannot turn all of its input heat into work.","steps":["An engine takes heat from a hot source.","It must reject some heat to a cold sink.","Only the difference is available as work.","So no engine working in a cycle can convert all the heat it takes in into work."],"others":"Needing a hot source is true but is not the second law. Saying no engine can reject heat is wrong; rejecting heat is exactly what every engine does. Saying heat can never flow from hot to cold is false: it flows that way naturally.","trap":"Efficiency 100% is impossible, even for a perfect engine.","answer":"no engine working in a cycle can convert all the heat it takes from a hot source into work"},"phy-12-04-m14":{"idea":"The Clausius statement is about the direction of heat flow. Heat does not go uphill by itself.","steps":["Heat flows naturally from a hot body to a cold body.","To move it from cold to hot, something must do work.","A refrigerator does this with a compressor.","So heat cannot flow from cold to hot without work."],"others":"Heat does flow from hot to cold; that is the natural direction. A refrigerator gives out more heat than the work put in, so the claim in the second wrong choice is reversed. Work can turn into heat fully in many ways, not only by friction.","trap":"Kelvin–Planck is about engines; Clausius is about refrigerators.","answer":"heat cannot flow from a colder body to a hotter body without work being done"},"phy-12-04-m15":{"idea":"Efficiency is the useful work divided by the heat taken in.","steps":["The work in one cycle is W = Q_H − Q_C.","Efficiency η = W/Q_H.","η = (Q_H − Q_C)/Q_H = 1 − Q_C/Q_H.","The ratio has no unit and is less than 1."],"others":"Q_C/Q_H is the fraction wasted, not the fraction used. 1 − Q_H/Q_C would be negative, since Q_H is larger. Q_H − Q_C is the work in joules, not the efficiency.","trap":"Efficiency is a pure number, so any answer with a joule unit cannot be right.","answer":"1 − Q_C/Q_H"},"phy-12-04-m16":{"idea":"A Carnot engine's efficiency depends only on the two temperatures, which must be in kelvin.","steps":["For a Carnot cycle, Q_C/Q_H = T_C/T_H.","Efficiency η = 1 − Q_C/Q_H.","So η = 1 − T_C/T_H.","T_H is the hot temperature and T_C is the cold temperature."],"others":"T_C/T_H is the fraction wasted, not the efficiency. (T_H − T_C)/T_C can be bigger than 1 and so is not an efficiency. 1 − T_H/T_C is negative.","trap":"Efficiency is always below 1, so check that your formula gives that.","answer":"1 − T_C/T_H"},"phy-12-04-m17":{"idea":"The Carnot efficiency formula tells us when 100% efficiency would happen.","steps":["η = 1 − T_C/T_H.","η = 1 only if T_C/T_H = 0.","That needs T_C = 0 K, a sink at absolute zero.","Absolute zero cannot be reached, so 100% is never achieved."],"others":"The working substance does not change the Carnot efficiency, so an ideal gas gives nothing special. A source at 0 K would give a negative or meaningless efficiency. Equal temperatures give η = 0, the worst case.","trap":"Wider temperature gap means higher efficiency.","answer":"the sink were at 0 K"},"phy-12-04-m18":{"idea":"A refrigerator's coefficient of performance is how much heat it removes per unit of work.","steps":["For a Carnot refrigerator, K = T_C/(T_H − T_C).","T_C = 270 K and T_H = 300 K.","K = 270/(300 − 270) = 270/30.","So the coefficient of performance is K = 9."],"others":"0.1 turns the fraction upside down (30/270). 10 uses 300/30, the wrong top number. 30 is only the temperature difference.","trap":"The top number is the cold temperature, because heat is removed from the cold space.","answer":"9"},"phy-12-04-m19":{"idea":"The coefficient of performance tells how much cooling you get for each joule of work you pay.","steps":["A refrigerator removes heat Q_C from the cold space.","It needs work W to do this.","K = Q_C/W.","K can be larger than 1, so it is not called an efficiency."],"others":"Heat given to the room divided by work is the heating figure of a heat pump. Heat removed divided by heat given to the room is below 1 and has no use here. Work divided by heat removed is the inverse of K.","trap":"Higher K means less electricity for the same cooling.","answer":"the heat removed from the cold space divided by the work done on the refrigerant"},"phy-12-04-m20":{"idea":"The second law sets an upper limit on efficiency. A reversible engine reaches it.","steps":["Between two fixed temperatures, no engine can beat a reversible engine.","All reversible engines between them have the same efficiency, 1 − T_C/T_H.","This value does not depend on what the working substance is.","So the most efficient engine is the reversible (Carnot) engine."],"others":"Size of working volume has no effect on the limit. Good insulation against friction helps but cannot make a real engine reversible. An ideal gas as the working substance is no better than any other substance in a reversible engine.","trap":"A real engine always has lower efficiency than a Carnot engine at the same temperatures.","answer":"a reversible (Carnot) engine, whatever its working substance"},"phy-12-04-m21":{"idea":"Work done by a gas at constant pressure is pressure times the change in volume.","steps":["W = ∫ p dV, and p does not change.","So W = p ∫ dV = p(V₂ − V₁).","On a p–V diagram, this is the area of a rectangle.","In SI units, p in pascal and V in m³ give W in joule."],"others":"pV₂ counts the whole volume instead of the change. p(V₁ + V₂) adds the volumes when it should subtract. (V₂ − V₁)/p divides by p and has the wrong units.","trap":"Always use the change in volume, not the volume itself.","answer":"p(V₂ − V₁)"},"phy-12-04-m22":{"idea":"The Stirling cycle is built from isothermal steps and constant-volume steps.","steps":["The gas expands isothermally at the high temperature and takes in heat.","It is cooled at constant volume.","It is compressed isothermally at the low temperature.","It is heated at constant volume to return to the start."],"others":"Two isothermal and two adiabatic steps make a Carnot cycle. Two isobaric and two isochoric steps is not a named heat-engine cycle. Two isobaric and two adiabatic steps is close to the Brayton cycle.","trap":"Carnot has adiabats; Stirling has isochores in their place.","answer":"two isothermal and two isochoric processes"},"phy-12-04-m23":{"idea":"The Carnot cycle is the ideal reversible cycle. It has only two kinds of step.","steps":["Step 1: isothermal expansion at T_H, taking in heat.","Step 2: adiabatic expansion, cooling from T_H to T_C.","Step 3: isothermal compression at T_C, rejecting heat.","Step 4: adiabatic compression, heating from T_C to T_H."],"others":"Two isothermal and two isochoric steps is the Stirling cycle. Two isobaric and two isochoric steps is not the Carnot cycle. Four adiabatic steps could not take in or reject any heat at all.","trap":"Isothermal steps are where heat moves; adiabatic steps change temperature.","answer":"two isothermal and two adiabatic processes"},"phy-12-04-m24":{"idea":"In an adiabatic process, pV^γ stays constant. This links pressure and volume.","steps":["pV^γ = constant, so p₁V₁^γ = p₂V₂^γ.","The volume is halved: V₂ = V₁/2.","p₂/p₁ = (V₁/V₂)^γ = 2^γ.","With γ = 5/3, p₂ = 2^(5/3) p₁, about 3.17 times p₁."],"others":"2 times the pressure is what an isothermal compression gives. 2^(2/3) and 2^(3/5) mix up the exponent. The exponent must be γ itself, which is 5/3.","trap":"Adiabatic compression raises pressure more than isothermal compression.","answer":"2^(5/3) times the initial pressure"},"phy-12-04-m25":{"idea":"Compressing a gas adds energy to it. If no heat leaves, that energy raises the temperature.","steps":["Adiabatic: Q = 0, so ΔU = −W.","In compression, the work done by the gas W is negative.","So ΔU is positive.","For an ideal gas, a rise in U means a rise in T."],"others":"It does not fall; work adds energy. It does not stay constant: that would be an isothermal compression with heat leaving. The temperature does not rise and then fall, because there is no heat to carry the energy away.","trap":"A bicycle pump gets warm for this reason.","answer":"rises, since the work done on the gas increases its internal energy"},"phy-12-04-n01":{"idea":"The first law relates heat, work and the change in internal energy.","steps":["ΔU = Q − W.","Heat absorbed Q = 800 J and work done by the gas W = 300 J.","ΔU = 800 − 300 = 500 J.","The internal energy rises by 500 J."],"trap":"Heat in is positive; work done by the gas is positive and is subtracted.","answer":"500 J"},"phy-12-04-n02":{"idea":"At constant pressure, work is pressure times the change in volume.","steps":["W = p(V₂ − V₁).","p = 2 × 10⁵ Pa. The volume change is 8 − 3 = 5 L = 5 × 10⁻³ m³.","W = 2 × 10⁵ × 5 × 10⁻³.","W = 1000 J."],"trap":"Convert litres to m³ first: 1 L = 10⁻³ m³.","answer":"1000 J"},"phy-12-04-n03":{"idea":"Isothermal expansion work uses a natural logarithm of the volume ratio.","steps":["W = nRT ln(V₂/V₁).","n = 2, R = 8.314, T = 300 K, so nRT = 4988.4 J.","V₂/V₁ = 40/10 = 4 and ln 4 = 1.3863.","W = 4988.4 × 1.3863 ≈ 6915 J."],"trap":"The ratio of volumes is enough, so litres need no conversion here.","answer":"6915 J"},"phy-12-04-n04":{"idea":"In an adiabatic process, we find the final pressure from pV^γ = constant, then use the work formula.","steps":["p_f = p_i (V_i/V_f)^γ = 10⁵ × (1/2)^(5/3) = 3.150 × 10⁴ Pa.","p_i V_i = 10⁵ × 2 × 10⁻³ = 200 J.","p_f V_f = 3.150 × 10⁴ × 4 × 10⁻³ = 126.0 J.","W = (p_i V_i − p_f V_f)/(γ − 1) = (200 − 126.0)/(2/3).","W = 74.0 × 1.5 = 111.0 J."],"trap":"The work done is positive because the gas expands, and ΔU = −W is negative.","answer":"111.0 J"},"phy-12-04-n05":{"idea":"Carnot efficiency depends only on the source and sink temperatures.","steps":["η = 1 − T_C/T_H.","T_H = 500 K and T_C = 300 K.","η = 1 − 300/500 = 1 − 0.6 = 0.4.","η = 40%."],"trap":"Use kelvin, and put the cold temperature on top.","answer":"40%"},"phy-12-04-n06":{"idea":"First find the Carnot efficiency, then multiply by the heat taken in.","steps":["η = 1 − T_C/T_H = 1 − 400/800 = 0.5.","W = η Q_H.","Q_H = 2000 J.","W = 0.5 × 2000 = 1000 J."],"trap":"Check: Q_C = 2000 × 400/800 = 1000 J, and Q_H − Q_C = 1000 J.","answer":"1000 J"},"phy-12-04-n07":{"idea":"Efficiency is work divided by the heat taken in. The work is the heat difference.","steps":["W = Q_H − Q_C = 600 − 450 = 150 J.","η = W/Q_H.","η = 150/600 = 0.25.","η = 25%."],"answer":"25%"},"phy-12-04-n08":{"idea":"The coefficient of performance of a Carnot refrigerator comes from its two temperatures.","steps":["K = T_C/(T_H − T_C).","T_C = 250 K inside and T_H = 300 K in the room.","T_H − T_C = 50 K.","K = 250/50 = 5."],"trap":"Check: with Q_C = 1000 J, Q_H = 1200 J, so W = 200 J and K = 1000/200 = 5.","answer":"5"},"phy-12-04-n09":{"idea":"Use K to find the heat removed. Then add the work to find the heat given to the room.","steps":["K = Q_C/W, so Q_C = K × W = 5 × 1000 = 5000 J.","This is the heat taken from the inside.","Energy is conserved: Q_H = Q_C + W.","Q_H = 5000 + 1000 = 6000 J."],"trap":"The room gets more heat than was removed, because the work turns into heat too.","answer":"6000 J"},"phy-12-04-n10":{"idea":"At constant pressure, the heat needed uses C_p. For a diatomic gas C_p = (7/2)R.","steps":["C_v = (5/2)R, so C_p = C_v + R = (7/2)R.","Q = n C_p ΔT.","n = 1 mol and ΔT = 400 − 300 = 100 K.","Q = 3.5 × 8.314 × 100 = 2909.9 ≈ 2910 J."],"trap":"Check: ΔU = (5/2)R × 100 = 2078.5 J, and W = RΔT = 831.4 J. They add to 2910 J.","answer":"2910 J"},"phy-12-04-n11":{"idea":"For a closed loop, the net work is the area inside the loop on the p–V diagram.","steps":["The loop is a rectangle.","Pressure difference Δp = 3 × 10⁵ − 1 × 10⁵ = 2 × 10⁵ Pa.","Volume difference ΔV = 5 − 1 = 4 L = 4 × 10⁻³ m³.","W = Δp × ΔV = 2 × 10⁵ × 4 × 10⁻³ = 800 J."],"trap":"If the loop runs clockwise on the diagram, the net work is positive.","answer":"800 J"}};
